/* ============================================================================
   digit-sum.epher -- the sum of the digits of a number

   Adds the digits of n with a packed while loop (guide 1.30): each pass
   takes the last digit with mod(n, 10), adds it to the running total, and
   drops it with floor(n / 10). The loop is silent, so labelled prints
   show the sums. The classic check: the digits of 12345 add to 15.

   Source: standard number theory; digit sums underlie divisibility by 3
   and 9 (the casting out of nines, Hardy and Wright, ch. 9) and feed the
   digital root of the next script. All results are exact.

   Run it from a terminal:
     Linux (deb, rpm):  epher /usr/lib/epher/scripts/number-theory/integer-sequences/digit-sum.epher
     Windows:           epher "$env:LOCALAPPDATA\epher\scripts\number-theory\integer-sequences\digit-sum.epher"
     macOS:             epher /Applications/epher.app/Contents/Resources/scripts/number-theory/integer-sequences/digit-sum.epher
   In the REPL:             load one of the paths above
   Web/desktop:             paste the whole file into the entry, press Enter

   There is no knob: the sample numbers are the point of the script.
   ============================================================================ */

// ---- sum the digits of 12345 --------------------------------------------------------------
// v = {remaining digits, running sum}; the loop ends when nothing is
// left, leaving the total in v[2].
v = {12345, 0}
while v[1] > 0 do v = {floor(v[1] / 10), v[2] + mod(v[1], 10)}
print("digit sum of 12345:", v[2])          // 1 + 2 + 3 + 4 + 5 = 15

// ---- a few more ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
// The same loop shape for other values; 9999 shows why the rule of
// casting out nines cares about the digit sum: it is 9 * 4 = 36.
v = {9999, 0}
while v[1] > 0 do v = {floor(v[1] / 10), v[2] + mod(v[1], 10)}
print("digit sum of 9999:", v[2])
v = {2026, 0}
while v[1] > 0 do v = {floor(v[1] / 10), v[2] + mod(v[1], 10)}
print("digit sum of 2026:", v[2])           // 2 + 0 + 2 + 6 = 10
print("12345 mod 9:", mod(12345, 9))        // 15 mod 9 = 6, the old check
/* ---- expected output ----
= {12345, 0}
= digit sum of 12345: 15
= {9999, 0}
= digit sum of 9999: 36
= {2026, 0}
= digit sum of 2026: 10
= 12345 mod 9: 6
*/
